  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1148 PM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThere have been only minimal changes to the forecast over the last \n24 hours. The Denver metro and the plains will see high \ntemperatures in the 70s through the week with high temperatures \neven pushing 80 on the eastern plains for Wednesday and Thursday. \nSome record warm minimums are possible for Denver towards the end \nof the week as cloud cover increases.\n\nRadar over the last several hours has shown some isolated to \nscattered rain/snow showers for the mountains, but there is almost \nno precipitation left at this hour. An upper-level ridge builds into \neastern Colorado on Tuesday and Wednesday with our area on the \nperiphery of it and some drier air filtering in. Wednesday and\nFriday should be the warmest days of the week ahead. Rain chances\nstart to decrease, even compared to the relatively low chances we\nhad on Monday. Tuesday only brings the chance for isolated \nrain/snow showers for the mountains. The coverage drops to near \nzero on Wednesday until late in the afternoon. Almost no \naccumulation is expected through at least 18z Wednesday. By \nWednesday afternoon, more widespread showers should start to \napproach our southwestern counties.\n\nThe overall synoptic pattern forecast through Friday generally \nremains the same as noted in previous discussions. An upper-level \nlow over the Pacific Ocean should approach California on Wednesday, \nmove inland, and be centered over Utah by Friday morning. Southwest \nflow aloft looks to continue through the week for our area with \nmoisture starting to increase on Wednesday. Some moisture \nassociated with what is now Tropical Storm Trudy gets pulled into \nthe flow aloft and funneled into southwest Colorado for late \nWednesday through Friday. The vast majority of the precipitation for \nour area should stay in the high country, but there is a a low \nchance a couple of showers could move onto the plains late Thursday. \nAs such, we now are forecasting little to no rainfall for the Denver \nmetro and the plains. Ensemble data remains in good agreement that \nthe highest rainfall amounts for our area will be in the southern \nhigh country (Park and Summit counties) where 0.25\" to 0.50\" looks \nreasonable with perhaps some localized slightly higher amounts. The \nrest of the high country likely sees 0.25\" or less. The system has \ntrended warmer, so only light snow accumulations at most are \npossible mainly above 12,000 ft. \n\nThere is some more agreement for this package on the evolution of \nthe trough for Friday and beyond. It now looks like the trough could \nsplit into two with part of it forming into a low near the Four \nCorners with the other part continuing to move off to the north and \neast late Friday through the weekend. A cold front should move \nthrough the forecast area early Saturday dropping high temperatures \nback to near-average or even below-average for Saturday through the \nbeginning of next week. It will start to feel more like fall once \nagain! Precipitation chances look to continue into the weekend, \nmainly on Saturday, with the highest chances once again for the high \ncountry and perhaps the Palmer Divide given an upslope component \nthere behind the cold front.",
